#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186187910 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "111da4c7-612b-5e1f-a105-2dbaaea4e5a5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Polar Curves Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/id #uuid "deaef5cc-99fb-57e5-a6f2-22f17a7f00af" :question/math-academy-id "e-10033" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/bdeef265fec0703b42b8d04e55502da49e8e84624a761368edd1dd5c106c7dbc.png)\n\nFind $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r =-2 \\cos\\theta,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=-2\\cos\\theta,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\sin2\\theta = 2\\sin\\theta\\cos\\theta.$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate $x'(\\theta)$ and $y'(\\theta).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(-2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188128 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14b618a5-02d2-5564-a288-3423a873a746" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "366bcb1d-3fe4-5047-8306-bd28935b5431" :question/math-academy-id "e-631" :question/problem "Calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d^2 y}{\\textrm d x^2}$ for the polar curve $r=5.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}$ and $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}x}{\\mathrm{d}\\theta}.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx = r \\cos \\theta, \\qquad y = r \\sin \\theta\n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta.$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=5,$ we get\n\n$$\nx =5\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y=5\\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta) = -5\\sin\\theta, \\qquad y'(\\theta) = 5\\cos\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{ 5\\cos\\theta }{  -5\\sin\\theta} =-\\cot\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353857 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "111da4c7-612b-5e1f-a105-2dbaaea4e5a5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Polar Curves Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7abd10da-5e70-58bb-8ce1-ce396915d3dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353859} {:answer/id #uuid "c3080c9f-a4cd-51f3-a1c6-a822753ac6b2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353860} {:answer/id #uuid "a18be4bd-b20e-5a94-aa12-9b012b0b40ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353861} {:answer/id #uuid "b5fa16ce-3fe3-5688-b010-a715081e1b60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353862} {:answer/id #uuid "b39effd7-1b8d-5978-9a63-4aafbfc154d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353863}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a18be4bd-b20e-5a94-aa12-9b012b0b40ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353861} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4e4718f-6d72-5bd9-b61d-0f48637e40a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0994f413-e2b9-5c97-b6f4-6123b264449e" :question/math-academy-id "q-141051" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/6c07657992f5c4f708cd7546902c5e26206bfa41f291a68ec7b582cb18a167fe.png)\n\nConsider the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,$ shown above. Given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +a\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +b\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\n$$\n\nfind the value of $a-b.$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$ \n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′}+(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2)+2(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & ={(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′}+{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )+(-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, $a=2,b=-2,$ and so \n\n$$\na-b=2-(-2)=4.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353864 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "111da4c7-612b-5e1f-a105-2dbaaea4e5a5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Polar Curves Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c1fd52-08ec-523e-95df-b3c3438cfc9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353866} {:answer/id #uuid "e0b5bad0-e161-50fa-a852-2ee776450536" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353867} {:answer/id #uuid "021b01d4-e94d-55c0-a5b4-c44fec4d9952" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡3\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353868} {:answer/id #uuid "67585bfe-0222-5ed4-ad60-df965c64182a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡3\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353869} {:answer/id #uuid "84c4e1d0-3be4-5df8-9a87-209974dfec94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353870}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07c1fd52-08ec-523e-95df-b3c3438cfc9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353866} :answer-field/id #uuid "83e98a1c-62e8-5aa5-98f6-d19124dce91d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fbfd5e83-8cab-5c90-baee-149584550131" :question/math-academy-id "q-142436" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/c7fd7d815bd006c27be4fc793e4cdc8c66ccd119d86167ad7f7cb1da64a4f2bd.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta , \\\\ y & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "111da4c7-612b-5e1f-a105-2dbaaea4e5a5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Polar Curves Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d1153339-2fb7-555d-a58e-d1f373bd6fe3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353873} {:answer/id #uuid "2a3bdd53-9513-5fbc-baf8-5eb19e45a955" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353874} {:answer/id #uuid "8c7fdcde-b145-5ed3-aabe-9252342cb400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353875} {:answer/id #uuid "fc1d1cbc-8b27-5509-949d-b4eb05d2eda8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353876} {:answer/id #uuid "bcbc3e60-d052-57eb-b278-e1c87936a09c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c7fdcde-b145-5ed3-aabe-9252342cb400" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353875} :answer-field/id #uuid "83c55745-63d9-52a0-9e4a-891f0ce9d2e2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b7527450-d1ad-5e91-b475-4c97c8ae425f" :question/math-academy-id "q-142437" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/5bbb314322763dad82b0390e9d9713ab551de16208a2eb0b75c4ceb3c1f9c611.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =(-5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =-10\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(-\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14b618a5-02d2-5564-a288-3423a873a746" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6e9ef96d-08df-5e6b-abf2-4777e0774cc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353880} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8ad0f5-17f7-5ae5-871b-d8314801e694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353881} {:answer/id #uuid "91c67e31-5a51-5d9a-8d60-30670ecbdd6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353882} {:answer/id #uuid "f82c6ff7-252e-5578-a5ee-0152f8901850" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\,{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353883} {:answer/id #uuid "1bb7a152-594e-55a0-a7af-2fe0817da81b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353884}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc8ad0f5-17f7-5ae5-871b-d8314801e694" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353881} :answer-field/id #uuid "44de3529-1272-58ca-8521-29909d1699ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c42ccfa7-152a-506f-8477-7897e821f9a9" :question/math-academy-id "q-149909" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for the polar curve $r=-\\sqrt{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=-\\sqrt{2},$ we get\n\n$$\nx=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta )}{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\,{csc}^{3}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14b618a5-02d2-5564-a288-3423a873a746" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79fa7c1a-1bdb-5eef-929c-682f0656b5a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353887} {:answer/id #uuid "11d05bed-2da2-5c2f-926a-6f5bcd9da16f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353888} {:answer/id #uuid "38190406-9000-5213-98b2-98e57409569c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353889} {:answer/id #uuid "4a32592d-df1b-5389-9f91-59c7b316edd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353890} {:answer/id #uuid "448a4df4-fde4-5047-83e7-451cfd5b472f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353891}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79fa7c1a-1bdb-5eef-929c-682f0656b5a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353887} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5458ebc-93b6-5418-b0c4-548bbf0a2cda" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353886}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9b959ff6-329f-5767-b830-663095c42098" :question/math-academy-id "q-149913" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for the polar curve $r=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ))}{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353892 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14b618a5-02d2-5564-a288-3423a873a746" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00ce826b-1a03-5e05-a993-0e8e5e8fec3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\sec(2\\theta)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353894}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00ce826b-1a03-5e05-a993-0e8e5e8fec3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}\\sec(2\\theta)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353894} :answer-field/id #uuid "46fb9669-d1f3-5edd-87dd-8618c92c7f74" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353893}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d2b2635-adb8-5bbf-a355-c4988487fdaf" :question/math-academy-id "q-348139" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ calculate $\\frac{{d}^{2}y}{d{x}^{2}}$ as a function of $\\theta .$\n\n$\\frac{{d}^{2}y}{d{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{d}^{2}y}{d{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}$ and $\\frac{dx}{d\\theta }.$ \n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nas parametric equations of a curve in terms of $\\theta .$ \n\nUsing the fact that $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,y=3{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nThen, to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ we use the rule\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}.\n$$\n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ))}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353895 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "111da4c7-612b-5e1f-a105-2dbaaea4e5a5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Polar Curves Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3266f4f4-9bfd-5ff4-968e-1e177a81ed3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3266f4f4-9bfd-5ff4-968e-1e177a81ed3b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353897} :answer-field/id #uuid "00f4488c-56d1-5964-9a35-6668c737bcc2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353896} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1294c89c-1661-5203-94ba-cf8540c2c607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1294c89c-1661-5203-94ba-cf8540c2c607" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353899} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5ccbd8e-f3d7-5c0b-ace8-b72a8f0c81fb" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b21b8bfe-2104-5b14-b103-89256b6fdf9c" :question/math-academy-id "q-352489" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/50ed8b3093adf431f41e6332fa43ac122df79c98d487961037443c77b4290fb7.png)\n\nFor the polar curve $r=6-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above, calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as a function of $\\theta .$ Fill in the missing parts in the result below.\n\n| $\\text{d}y$ | $=$ | {{field-1}}\n$\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\text{d}x$ |  | $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ |" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=6-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(6-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =(6-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ).$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =6{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{′}-{({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =6(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′} \\\\  & =6{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{′}-\\frac{1}{2}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}^{′} \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353900 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14b618a5-02d2-5564-a288-3423a873a746" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62aa50d2-5e2f-5e01-8df3-c9c6e67c714f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\sec(2\\theta)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353902} {:answer/id #uuid "a623aa1d-615d-5c1e-a227-aa7faf810f24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\sec(2\\theta)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62aa50d2-5e2f-5e01-8df3-c9c6e67c714f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}\\sec(2\\theta)^3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353902} :answer-field/id #uuid "b32a729c-a2f0-57ec-b9e8-155e3cfc0ead" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353901}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8bf1a73a-f9d5-555d-b905-2871dec37111" :question/math-academy-id "q-352518" :question/problem "For the polar curve $r=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ as a function of $\\theta $ given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$\n\n$\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ))}{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sec}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )}{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}{sec}^{3}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "14b618a5-02d2-5564-a288-3423a873a746" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Second Derivative of a Polar Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e774d92-278f-5440-a0ee-fee9c540e35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353906} {:answer/id #uuid "8dc52bf5-5ac6-515b-8277-5b52fec278b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353907} {:answer/id #uuid "b6576735-38d4-5362-9a1f-79a446c765bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{csc}^{2}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353908} {:answer/id #uuid "5a7e9a5c-f47a-5993-a5c7-f3c9bb833603" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353909} {:answer/id #uuid "cdc2babb-08be-5ee1-9fa8-4dc5e11073e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e774d92-278f-5440-a0ee-fee9c540e35d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353906} :answer-field/id #uuid "70d14fb6-449c-5b80-9ad2-200f17169bf5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5b9b7a9-423e-5b3c-bf6d-ada38829b4e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-75295" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}$ for the polar curve $r=2,$ given that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ).$" :question/worked-solution "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(\\frac{dy}{dx})}{(\\frac{dx}{d\\theta })}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ).$ Also, since $r=2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dx}{d\\theta } & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting into our formula for the second derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\frac{d}{d\\theta }(-\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\theta ))}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{csc}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}{-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{csc}^{3}⁡(\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "111da4c7-612b-5e1f-a105-2dbaaea4e5a5" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Polar Curves Using the Double-Angle Formula for Sine"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bd928b77-2c02-501f-b255-ecab853a114f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\cot 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187899} {:answer/id #uuid "d3bec273-088e-5276-8ed7-1d14cc7e27f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\tan 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187900} {:answer/id #uuid "f8c50b47-5ff2-536a-9173-bf87819ae207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cot 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187901} {:answer/id #uuid "4eb08f01-94a9-5212-8871-2c917027ca81" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\tan 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187902} {:answer/id #uuid "699f899d-e6c7-5b85-ab74-0bb458e45a25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\tan θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187903}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8c50b47-5ff2-536a-9173-bf87819ae207" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\cot 2θ" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187901} :answer-field/id #uuid "c088d0f4-0714-5f57-bbfb-380defa14744" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186187898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a7256ac7-6551-5109-b9b8-6e1497bbcfbe" :question/math-academy-id "q-9388" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14065566/assets/2edd37e6318a7d78b3e6f496bb3f688d006949ccab983f75cece9993817f6b69.png)\n\nFind $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ for the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "To find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use the formula for differentiating parametric curves:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )}\n$$\n\nFirst, we recall that to convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $r=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta , \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere we used the identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTo compute our derivative, we first need to calculate ${x}^{′}(\\theta )$ and ${y}^{′}(\\theta ):$ \n\n- First, we compute the derivative of $x$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\theta ) & =({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{′} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Then, we compute the derivative of $y$ with respect to $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta ) & ={(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta )}^{′} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta }{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cot}⁡2\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
